WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.100
I think early on we were our own customer

2
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.890
and so we had a lot of pain points.

3
00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:05.380
I think the next thing we did

4
00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.040
was we realized that we now had

5
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.220
reliable upload of a lot of telemetry

6
00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:10.960
that you can collect.

7
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.060
A lot of that data

8
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.380
was never being looked at, right?

9
00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.560
That's actually true of most observability systems,

10
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:17.440
like 90% of the data is never looked at.

11
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:18.490
It's only the bad conditions.

12
00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.600
And so why were we wasting all this bandwidth

13
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.180
and storage and ingest

14
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.150
for data we weren't looking at?

15
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.440
And so the next thing we did

16
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:28.440
was add a little bit

17
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:29.700
of processing at the edge.

18
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.410
So instead of saying,

19
00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:32.830
hey, I'm going to upload all these logs,

20
00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.100
maybe I just summarize

21
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:35.760
or metricize them at the edge.

22
00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.870
And so instead of megabytes of logs,

23
00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:39.770
I could send very small counters.

24
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.180
Saved a huge amount of bandwidth

25
00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:43.120
and really allowed us

26
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.540
to create metrics on the fly

27
00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.510
based on what was happening on the device.

28
00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:47.140
And so that was

29
00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:48.690
a really powerful feature we added.

30
00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:50.380
And then the next thing we did

31
00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:52.530
was making this all runtime configurable.

32
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:56.560
Now, most observability systems make decisions at compile time.

33
00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.770
You add a logline, you add a metric,

34
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.060
and that is done

35
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:01.370
when you compile and then you release.

36
00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.540
You're doing 500 or 600 releases a day

37
00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:04.880
on the server side,

38
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:05.810
which is what we were.

39
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:07.050
That's not a problem.

40
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.370
That's like a few minutes or 10 minutes,

41
00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.870
like 20 minutes of delay.

42
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.460
On mobile, an average release cycle

43
00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.290
was anywhere between 10 and 15 days.

44
00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:17.670
You have your weekly trains,

45
00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.570
you have your alphas and betas the next week.

46
00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:21.010
Then you have to go to the App Store,

47
00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:22.170
you have to get it approved,

48
00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:23.300
and then it has to propagate

49
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:24.850
to everyone's customer devices.

50
00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:25.980
So by the time

51
00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.120
you hear about a customer problem

52
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.270
and add a logline,

53
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:29.160
all of a sudden

54
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.190
you had a really long delay.

55
00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:32.830
And God forbid you didn't get it right,

56
00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:33.890
you'd have to do that again.

57
00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:35.180
And then after all that,

58
00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:36.420
you'd have to turn it off at the end

59
00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:37.410
because it was so expensive.

60
00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:39.730
So the other thing we did,

61
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:40.540
and this is I think

62
00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:41.400
one of the really things

63
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.760
that unlocked a lot of the rest

64
00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:43.930
of what we do with Bitdrift,

65
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.800
is now we at runtime can say,

66
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:47.740
do I want this log or not?

67
00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.590
For these conditions, for these set of people,

68
00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:51.920
I can at runtime

69
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:53.500
choose what I want to do.

70
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.450
And so observability has now become runtime for mobile,

71
00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:56.410
which makes it really nice.

72
00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:57.570
You can say, I heard this problem,

73
00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:58.650
get more information,

74
00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:00.590
I don't need any more, turn it off.

75
00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:01.440
And that's something

76
00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:02.920
that used to take you weeks or months,

77
00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:03.540
and now you can do it

78
00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:04.150
in a few minutes.

79
00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:07.250
So you combined like the local storage,

80
00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:08.650
the ability to edge process,

81
00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:09.780
and the ability to just be able

82
00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:10.320
to turn it on

83
00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:11.930
and control it from runtime.

84
00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:13.520
We built that last part.

85
00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.870
That really is the core of the platform.

86
00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.040
And we've layered all the sort of

87
00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.190
traditional observability use cases on top.
